        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Briscolone',
            subtitle: 'Card game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Briscolone is a two-player trick-taking card game without a trump suit. Players follow suit when possible and use Briscola-style point values to win tricks and accumulate a running total over several deals.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Country/Region of Origin: Italy\nTime Period: Early 20th century\nHistorical Development: Briscolone evolved as a larger two-player variant of the Italian point-trick game Briscola, using the same card values but dealing more cards and removing the trump suit.\nCultural Significance: Played as a casual head-to-head game and described in modern Italian card-game collections.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Minimum Players: 2\nMaximum Players: 2\nOptimal Players: 2\nPartnerships: Not used; Briscolone is played head-to-head.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Type: Standard 52-card deck\nNumber of Decks: 1\nJokers: Not included',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card deck\nScore sheet (optional)\nTable space',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Cards per Player: Varies by player count\nDealing Pattern: Clockwise\n--',
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
